-    "content": "# [Guide] Three-Surface Model Context Protocol (MCP) Server Architecture\n\n## Overview\n\nThe Model Context Protocol (MCP) allows Large Language Models (LLMs) and autonomous engineering agents to interact with development platforms. However, conventional tracker integrations (such as legacy Jira REST bridges and generic CRUD wrappers) expose raw REST endpoints 1:1 as MCP tools. This naive approach results in 70 to 120 bloated tools consuming 15,000 to 25,000 context tokens before the agent begins reasoning, causing catastrophic context exhaustion and multi-turn thrashing.\n\n\ud83d\udc4dkanban rejects the CRUD-wrapper pattern. Instead, it implements an **Intent-Driven Architecture** organized into **three isolated streamable surfaces** (`2025-06-18` protocol specification), exposing exactly 27 cohesive tools designed specifically for autonomous agent cognition.\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"scope-tiers\"></a>\n## The Three Isolated MCP Surfaces\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    subgraph Hub[\"\ud83d\udc4dkanban Multi-Surface MCP Server\"]\n        Agent[\"/mcp/agent<br/>- 9 Execution & QA Tools<br/>- Token budget: <= 1,850 tokens<br/>- Zero Admin / Delete permissions\"]\n        Docs[\"/mcp/docs<br/>- 6 Knowledge & Spec Tools<br/>- Token budget: <= 1,350 tokens<br/>- Stickies & Hierarchical Docs\"]\n        Admin[\"/mcp/admin<br/>- 12 Lifecycle & Ops Tools<br/>- Token budget: <= 2,800 tokens<br/>- Mandatory AAL2 Protection<br/>- 2-Stage Destructive Confirmation\"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph Agents[\"Target Consumers\"]\n        CodingAgents[\"Autonomous Coding & QA Agents<br/>(Cursor, Claude Code, Antigravity, Codex)\"]\n        DocAgents[\"Documentation & Knowledge Bots\"]\n        AdminOperators[\"DevOps & Administrative Operators\"]\n    end\n\n    CodingAgents --> Agent\n    DocAgents --> Docs\n    AdminOperators --> Admin\n```\n\n![MCP Access Studio and Key Configuration](images/13-mcp-access-studio.png)\n\n![MCP Level of Detail Tiers Studio](images/21-mcp-lod-tiers-studio.png)\n\n### 1. `/mcp/agent` (9 Execution and QA Tools)\nDedicated to autonomous software development, testing, and QA agents (such as Claude Code, Antigravity, and Cursor).\n- **Security Constraint:** Strictly limited by the caller's role. Contains zero administrative, destructive, or project-deletion capabilities.\n- **Token Efficiency:** The entire tool manifest consumes only 1,759 tokens, comfortably fitting within the strict 1,850-token specification ceiling.\n- **Intent Consolidation:** Replaces multi-step CRUD sequences with atomic actions like `begin_work` (claims ticket, starts sprint, links active cycle, posts plan) and `complete_work` (verifies blockers, records QA proof, marks done).\n\n### 2. `/mcp/docs` (6 Knowledge and Specification Tools)\nDedicated to technical documentation, architecture research, and specification authoring.\n- **Capabilities:** Document creation, ltree hierarchy traversal, markdown search, sticky note management, and AI-assisted sticky-to-doc promotion.\n- **Token Efficiency:** Tool manifest utilizes 1,177 tokens against a 1,350-token budget ceiling.\n\n### 3. `/mcp/admin` (12 Governance and Lifecycle Tools)\nDedicated to platform administrators and privileged automation operators.\n- **Capabilities:** Project provisioning, member and group access binding, workflow transition helper configuration, AI runner setup, and system health checks.\n- **AAL2 Protection:** Every invocation requires Authenticator Assurance Level 2 credentials.\n- **Two-Stage Destructive Safety:** Dangerous operations (`destructive_lifecycle`) require a two-stage process: first requesting a blast-radius preview and receiving a short-lived cryptographic confirmation token, then explicitly confirming execution with that token.\n\n---\n\n## Token Optimization and Serializer Architecture\n\nAutonomous agents run faster and make fewer mistakes when data payloads are clean, predictable, and compact.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    Request[\"Agent Invocation<br/>e.g. read_ticket(identifier='KAN-42')\"] --> Auth[\"Auth & Scope Check\"]\n    Auth --> Resolver[\"EntityResolver<br/>(Translate KAN-42 to UUID)\"]\n    Resolver --> DB[\"PostgreSQL Engine<br/>FORCE RLS Active\"]\n    DB --> LODFilter[\"LOD Filter<br/>(summary / standard / full)\"]\n    LODFilter --> Dumper[\"InlineSafeDumper<br/>Strip nulls, compact YAML\"]\n    Dumper --> Response[\"Streamable Response<br/>(-35% Tokens vs JSON)\"]\n```\n\n### `InlineSafeDumper` Compact YAML Output\nWhile standard MCP servers return formatted JSON, \ud83d\udc4dkanban uses a specialized YAML serializer (`mcp/yaml_out.py`):\n- Compact, human-readable YAML representation.\n- Strips null values, empty strings, and default boolean flags.\n- Automatically redacts sensitive fields (passwords, tokens, keys).\n- **Result:** Reduces payload size by 35% compared to raw JSON, saving thousands of tokens per conversation.\n\n### `EntityResolver` Human-Readable Identifiers\nAgents reason far better about human identifiers (`KAN-42`, `CORE-101`, `infra-docs`) than opaque UUIDs (`01932e6b-7341-789a-bc01-123456789abc`). The `EntityResolver` automatically translates between human keys and internal database UUIDs at the MCP boundary.\n\n### Level-of-Detail (`lod`) Filtering\nSearch and read tools support a progressive disclosure parameter:\n- `lod: \"summary\"`: Returns identifier, title, state, and priority (~40 tokens).\n- `lod: \"standard\"`: Returns summary plus description and primary assignees (~200 tokens).\n- `lod: \"full\"`: Returns complete object graph, all comments, activity history, and access manifests.\n\n---\n\n## MCP Access Studio and Key Management\n\nUsers and administrators configure MCP integrations through the visual Access Studio at `/admin/mcp` and `/settings/mcp`.\n\n![MCP Access Studio and Key Configuration](images/13-mcp-access-studio.png)\n\n### Studio Features\n- **Granular Tool Toggles:** Enable or disable individual MCP tools per key or per surface.\n- **Hierarchical Scopes and Scope Badges:** Restrict tokens to `Global`, `Workspace`, or single `Project` scopes with distinct visual badges indicating authorization level.\n- **RFC 8628 Device Flow (`/activate`):** Fast terminal onboarding allowing agents to authenticate via browser activation code.\n- **Copyable Configuration Snippets:** Real-time generation of configuration snippets for Claude Desktop (`claude_desktop_config.json`), Google Antigravity (`agy`), Cursor, and OpenAI Codex.\n\n![MCP Access Studio Scope Badges](images/59-mcp-scope-badge.png)\n\n![MCP Mint Key and Client Snippets Modal](images/58-mcp-client-snippets-mint.png)\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"oauth-21--pkce\"></a>\n<a id=\"oauth-21-pkce\"></a>\n## Admin OAuth 2.1 Governance Studio (`/admin/oauth`)\n\nIn addition to static API keys, \ud83d\udc4dkanban provides an enterprise OAuth 2.1 authorization server with mandatory PKCE (Proof Key for Code Exchange with `S256` challenge), accessible at `/admin/oauth`.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    subgraph OAuthGovernance[\"OAuth 2.1 & PKCE Architecture\"]\n        ClientApp[\"Third-Party Client / IDE Agent<br/>(Cursor, Claude Desktop, Antigravity)\"]\n        AuthServer[\"\ud83d\udc4dkanban OAuth 2.1 Server (/admin/oauth)\"]\n        TokenEndpoint[\"Token Minting Endpoint (/api/v1/oauth/token)\"]\n        AdminAudit[\"Audit & Revocation Stream\"]\n\n        ClientApp -->|1. Authorization Code Request + code_challenge (S256)| AuthServer\n        AuthServer -->|2. User Authenticates & Approves Scopes| ClientApp\n        ClientApp -->|3. POST /oauth/token + code_verifier| TokenEndpoint\n        TokenEndpoint -->|4. Cryptographic S256 Verification| TokenEndpoint\n        TokenEndpoint -->>|5. Mint Scoped Bearer JWT + Refresh Token| ClientApp\n        TokenEndpoint -->|6. Append Registration & Issuance Record| AdminAudit\n    end\n```\n\n![Admin OAuth Client Governance Studio](images/42-admin-oauth-governance.png)\n\n### Key OAuth Governance Capabilities:\n1. **Registered OAuth 2.1 Clients:** View, register, and manage client applications with designated redirect URIs and client secrets.\n2. **Mandatory PKCE (`S256`):** Disallows plain code challenges, protecting public clients against authorization code interception attacks.\n3. **Granular Scopes:** Scopes are scoped strictly (`mcp:read`, `mcp:write`, `agent:execute`, `docs:read`, `admin:full`).\n4. **Immediate Client Revocation:** Administrators can revoke compromised OAuth clients or reset client secrets with zero system downtime.\n"
+    "content": "# [Guide] Three-Surface Model Context Protocol (MCP) Server Architecture\n\n## Overview\n\nThe Model Context Protocol (MCP) allows Large Language Models (LLMs) and autonomous engineering agents to interact with development platforms. However, conventional tracker integrations (such as legacy Jira REST bridges and generic CRUD wrappers) expose raw REST endpoints 1:1 as MCP tools. This naive approach results in 70 to 120 bloated tools consuming 15,000 to 25,000 context tokens before the agent begins reasoning, causing catastrophic context exhaustion and multi-turn thrashing.\n\n\ud83d\udc4dkanban rejects the CRUD-wrapper pattern. Instead, it implements an **Intent-Driven Architecture** organized into **three isolated streamable surfaces** (`2025-06-18` protocol specification), exposing exactly 24 cohesive tools (9 `/mcp/agent`, 3 `/mcp/docs`, 12 `/mcp/admin`) designed specifically for autonomous agent cognition.\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"scope-tiers\"></a>\n## The Three Isolated MCP Surfaces\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    subgraph Hub[\"\ud83d\udc4dkanban Multi-Surface MCP Server\"]\n        Agent[\"/mcp/agent<br/>- 9 Execution & QA Tools<br/>- Token budget: <= 1,850 tokens<br/>- Zero Admin / Delete permissions\"]\n        Docs[\"/mcp/docs<br/>- 3 Knowledge & Spec Tools<br/>- Token budget: <= 1,350 tokens<br/>- Search, Create, Update Docs\"]\n        Admin[\"/mcp/admin<br/>- 12 Lifecycle & Ops Tools<br/>- Token budget: <= 2,800 tokens<br/>- Mandatory AAL2 Protection<br/>- 2-Stage Destructive Confirmation\"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph Agents[\"Target Consumers\"]\n        CodingAgents[\"Autonomous Coding & QA Agents<br/>(Cursor, Claude Code, Antigravity, Codex)\"]\n        DocAgents[\"Documentation & Knowledge Bots\"]\n        AdminOperators[\"DevOps & Administrative Operators\"]\n    end\n\n    CodingAgents --> Agent\n    DocAgents --> Docs\n    AdminOperators --> Admin\n```\n\n![MCP Access Studio and Key Configuration](images/13-mcp-access-studio.png)\n\n![MCP Level of Detail Tiers Studio](images/21-mcp-lod-tiers-studio.png)\n\n### 1. `/mcp/agent` (9 Execution and QA Tools)\nDedicated to autonomous software development, testing, and QA agents (such as Claude Code, Antigravity, and Cursor).\n- **Security Constraint:** Strictly limited by the caller's role. Contains zero administrative, destructive, or project-deletion capabilities.\n- **Token Efficiency:** The entire tool manifest consumes only 1,759 tokens, comfortably fitting within the strict 1,850-token specification ceiling.\n- **Intent Consolidation:** Replaces multi-step CRUD sequences with atomic actions like `begin_work` (claims ticket, starts sprint, links active cycle, posts plan) and `complete_work` (verifies blockers, records QA proof, marks done).\n\n### 2. `/mcp/docs` (3 Knowledge and Specification Tools)\nDedicated to technical documentation, architecture research, and specification authoring.\n- **Capabilities:** Document creation, markdown search, and content updates (`search`, `create`, `update`).\n- **Token Efficiency:** Tool manifest utilizes under 800 tokens against a 1,350-token budget ceiling.\n\n### 3. `/mcp/admin` (12 Governance and Lifecycle Tools)\nDedicated to platform administrators and privileged automation operators.\n- **Capabilities:** Project provisioning, member and group access binding, workflow transition helper configuration, AI runner setup, and system health checks.\n- **AAL2 Protection:** Every invocation requires Authenticator Assurance Level 2 credentials.\n- **Two-Stage Destructive Safety:** Dangerous operations (`destructive_lifecycle`) require a two-stage process: first requesting a blast-radius preview and receiving a short-lived cryptographic confirmation token, then explicitly confirming execution with that token.\n\n---\n\n## Token Optimization and Serializer Architecture\n\nAutonomous agents run faster and make fewer mistakes when data payloads are clean, predictable, and compact.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    Request[\"Agent Invocation<br/>e.g. read_ticket(identifier='KAN-42')\"] --> Auth[\"Auth & Scope Check\"]\n    Auth --> Resolver[\"EntityResolver<br/>(Translate KAN-42 to UUID)\"]\n    Resolver --> DB[\"PostgreSQL Engine<br/>FORCE RLS Active\"]\n    DB --> LODFilter[\"LOD Filter<br/>(summary / standard / full)\"]\n    LODFilter --> Dumper[\"InlineSafeDumper<br/>Strip nulls, compact YAML\"]\n    Dumper --> Response[\"Streamable Response<br/>(-35% Tokens vs JSON)\"]\n```\n\n### `InlineSafeDumper` Compact YAML Output\nWhile standard MCP servers return formatted JSON, \ud83d\udc4dkanban uses a specialized YAML serializer (`mcp/yaml_out.py`):\n- Compact, human-readable YAML representation.\n- Strips null values, empty strings, and default boolean flags.\n- Automatically redacts sensitive fields (passwords, tokens, keys).\n- **Result:** Reduces payload size by 35% compared to raw JSON, saving thousands of tokens per conversation.\n\n### `EntityResolver` Human-Readable Identifiers\nAgents reason far better about human identifiers (`KAN-42`, `CORE-101`, `infra-docs`) than opaque UUIDs (`01932e6b-7341-789a-bc01-123456789abc`). The `EntityResolver` automatically translates between human keys and internal database UUIDs at the MCP boundary.\n\n### Level-of-Detail (`lod`) Filtering\nSearch and read tools support a progressive disclosure parameter:\n- `lod: \"summary\"`: Returns identifier, title, state, and priority (~40 tokens).\n- `lod: \"standard\"`: Returns summary plus description and primary assignees (~200 tokens).\n- `lod: \"full\"`: Returns complete object graph, all comments, activity history, and access manifests.\n\n---\n\n## MCP Access Studio and Key Management\n\nUsers and administrators configure MCP integrations through the visual Access Studio at `/admin/mcp` and `/settings/mcp`.\n\n![MCP Access Studio and Key Configuration](images/13-mcp-access-studio.png)\n\n### Studio Features\n- **Granular Tool Toggles:** Enable or disable individual MCP tools per key or per surface.\n- **Hierarchical Scopes and Scope Badges:** Restrict tokens to `Global`, `Workspace`, or single `Project` scopes with distinct visual badges indicating authorization level.\n- **RFC 8628 Device Flow (`/activate`):** Fast terminal onboarding allowing agents to authenticate via browser activation code.\n- **Copyable Configuration Snippets:** Real-time generation of configuration snippets for Claude Desktop (`claude_desktop_config.json`), Google Antigravity (`agy`), Cursor, and OpenAI Codex.\n\n![MCP Access Studio Scope Badges](images/59-mcp-scope-badge.png)\n\n![MCP Mint Key and Client Snippets Modal](images/58-mcp-client-snippets-mint.png)\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"oauth-21--pkce\"></a>\n<a id=\"oauth-21-pkce\"></a>\n## Admin OAuth 2.1 Governance Studio (`/admin/oauth`)\n\nIn addition to static API keys, \ud83d\udc4dkanban provides an enterprise OAuth 2.1 authorization server with mandatory PKCE (Proof Key for Code Exchange with `S256` challenge), accessible at `/admin/oauth`.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    subgraph OAuthGovernance[\"OAuth 2.1 & PKCE Architecture\"]\n        ClientApp[\"Third-Party Client / IDE Agent<br/>(Cursor, Claude Desktop, Antigravity)\"]\n        AuthServer[\"\ud83d\udc4dkanban OAuth 2.1 Server (/admin/oauth)\"]\n        TokenEndpoint[\"Token Minting Endpoint (/api/v1/oauth/token)\"]\n        AdminAudit[\"Audit & Revocation Stream\"]\n\n        ClientApp -->|1. Authorization Code Request + code_challenge (S256)| AuthServer\n        AuthServer -->|2. User Authenticates & Approves Scopes| ClientApp\n        ClientApp -->|3. POST /oauth/token + code_verifier| TokenEndpoint\n        TokenEndpoint -->|4. Cryptographic S256 Verification| TokenEndpoint\n        TokenEndpoint -->>|5. Mint Scoped Bearer JWT + Refresh Token| ClientApp\n        TokenEndpoint -->|6. Append Registration & Issuance Record| AdminAudit\n    end\n```\n\n![Admin OAuth Client Governance Studio](images/42-admin-oauth-governance.png)\n\n### Key OAuth Governance Capabilities:\n1. **Registered OAuth 2.1 Clients:** View, register, and manage client applications with designated redirect URIs and client secrets.\n2. **Mandatory PKCE (`S256`):** Disallows plain code challenges, protecting public clients against authorization code interception attacks.\n3. **Granular Scopes:** Scopes are scoped strictly (`mcp:read`, `mcp:write`, `agent:execute`, `docs:read`, `admin:full`).\n4. **Immediate Client Revocation:** Administrators can revoke compromised OAuth clients or reset client secrets with zero system downtime.\n"
